### Q5 (c) Most confused classes and grayscale

Both models confused **eosinophil and monocyte** most (12 mistakes each in the printed output). Grayscale makes this harder because stain colour is a main cue for separating white blood cells: eosinophils have bright red-orange granules while monocytes have a bluish-grey cytoplasm. After converting to grayscale only brightness remains, so the two large cells look similar in size, brightness and texture, and HOG and SIFT, which only use edges and brightness, cannot tell them apart well.

